Vendored: codeparrot/apps_metric
================================

This package vendors the compute logic behind the "codeparrot/apps_metric"
HF Hub metric (https://huggingface.co/spaces/codeparrot/apps_metric),
originally licensed Apache-2.0, per the same fully-vendored/hard-fork
posture documented in this repo's top-level NOTICE (self.ai licenses the
combined work under GPLv3; Apache-2.0 is one-way compatible).

Files and the exact upstream revision each was pulled from (space repo
`codeparrot/apps_metric`, main branch history at the time of vendoring,
2026-07-18):

  - utils.py         <- utils.py         @ 2a1d3d698c4e91ce7c6636000f6988be9abcac23
                                            ("hide debugging comment", 2022-08-09)
  - testing_util.py  <- testing_util.py  @ d4ef3dc20ee6eb4bf5362cde8fc9a45bc2bad549
                                            ("remove print", 2022-08-28)

Both files were byte-identical to the metric's live HEAD (commit
9877be0aa476d466e0cf9e252191b3614a13b7cb, "Update the metadata (#4)",
2023-06-06 — a README-only change) at the time of vendoring; they have not
changed since 2022. Fetched directly via the HF Hub raw-file endpoint
(`https://huggingface.co/spaces/codeparrot/apps_metric/raw/<rev>/<file>`),
diffed against HEAD to confirm byte-for-byte match.

We did NOT vendor the upstream `apps_metric.py` wrapper (the
`evaluate.EvaluationModule` subclass HF's `evaluate.load()` dynamically
downloads and instantiates). That wrapper's only job is adapting
`compute_metrics()` to the `evaluate` library's plugin loader protocol; we
call `evaluate_generations()` / `get_results()` (from utils.py) directly
from code_eval/tasks/apps.py, so the wrapper is unnecessary. This is also
the entire point of vendoring: `evaluate.load("codeparrot/apps_metric")`
fetches `apps_metric.py` *live* from the Hub at runtime, so a change on the
Hub (or the Hub being unreachable) can silently change or break scoring.

Why this was vendored (self.code-eval issue #2, item 6):
`code_eval/tasks/apps.py::GeneralAPPS.process_results` used to call
`code_metric.compute(..., results_details=True)`, expecting the metric to
accept a `results_details` kwarg and return a `results` key with
per-problem/per-generation pass data to build `details` from.

That kwarg was never part of `apps_metric.py::_compute()` at ANY point in
its git history — verified across every commit that touched the file, from
the initial "Create new file" commit through the current HEAD
(`_compute` signatures seen, oldest to newest:
`(self, generations, k_list=[1,10,100], count_errors=True, level=["all"])` ->
`(self, generations, k_list=[1,10,100], count_errors=True, level="all")` ->
`(self, predictions, k_list=[1,10,100], count_errors=True, level="all", debug=False)`,
the last of these being HEAD since 2022-07-19). `compute_metrics()` /
`_compute()` only ever returns `{"avg_accuracy", "strict_accuracy",
"pass_at_k"}` — there has never been a `"results"` key either, so the old
`if "results" in results:` details-building block in apps.py was already
dead code regardless of the `results_details` TypeError.

`results_details=True` was introduced entirely on the self.ai side in
commit 8dbb2dc ("feat: add self.ai API integration and extend evaluation
tasks") without ever having existed upstream — i.e. it was never
"compatible with an older version," it was never compatible with any
version. There is no "revert to an old compatible revision" fix available;
the call site's expectations were wrong from the start.

The fix (this vendoring + the apps.py change that accompanies it) drops
`results_details` entirely and instead calls the vendored
`evaluate_generations()` directly to get the real raw per-problem,
per-generation test-case results, then builds `details` from that actual
data shape in apps.py — achieving the same intent (per-task pass/fail
detail alongside the aggregate metrics) the original `results_details=True`
call was reaching for, using the metric's real interface instead of an
imagined one.
